  - **Statement:** Rare, spatially concentrated clusters tend to align with extremes of large-scale spatial gene-expression gradients more often than not, but this association is heterogeneous across different spatial principal components and does not uniformly distinguish them from the most abundant diffuse clusters.  
  - **Grounded in:** OBSERVATIONs in `exploration_log.md` that (i) ranked all Leiden clusters by same-label neighbor enrichment to identify rare-but-tight communities, and (ii) summarized their spatial neighbor compositions and global abundances.  
  - **Test outcome:** REFINE — Recomputing concentration scores from the full spatial kNN graph highlighted rare-but-tight clusters with very high concentration scores (e.g., codes 26, 15, 20, 27, 29 with scores ≈60–81 despite global abundances ~0.7–1.4%). PCA on z-scored expression (10 PCs) showed that PC1 (r_x≈0.03, r_y≈–0.35) and PC2 (r_x≈–0.17, r_y≈0.68) were moderately aligned with spatial axes. Along PC1, the mean absolute cluster-level z-score was higher for rare concentrated clusters than for the three most abundant diffuse clusters (|z_mean| ≈0.93 vs 0.54), indicating that several rare communities sit near one tail of this spatial gradient. Along PC2, the pattern reversed (rare ≈0.73 vs diffuse ≈1.09), showing that abundant clusters can instead occupy spatial PC extremes on another axis. Overall, rare concentrated clusters are associated with gradient extremes for some spatial PCs but not universally across all, leading to a partial rather than global confirmation of the original hypothesis.  
  - **Quality (0–10):** Full numeric scores are stored in `hypotheses/hypotheses.json`. Qualitatively, this hypothesis is data-driven and testable, but its support is mixed across PCs, so its specificity and falsifiability apply more to a subset of spatial axes and clusters.

